      "evidence_type": "published tag insertion at this exact site",
      "functional_impact_measured": "Surface display confirmed (tag is extracellularly accessible); the paper reports the construct was engineered and used for surface labelling, but detailed functional assays (e.g. scramblase activity) are not described in the ledger clip.",
      "confidence": "high",
      "rationale": "The ledger line from DOI:10.1101/2025.06.08.658482 explicitly names Alanine 34 as the junction residue in a full-length, membrane-anchored TMEM123 construct. Python verification confirms: seq[33] = 'A' (Alanine, 1-indexed position 34), which is extracellular (EC span 27\u2013168), 135 residues from the TM boundary (169), and 8 residues after the mature N-terminus (27). residue_before = A (pos 34), residue_after = S (pos 35), both read directly from the numbered sequence. The signal peptide ends at residue 26 (authoritative landmark), so the tag at position 34 is correctly placed after SP cleavage and will not be removed co-translationally. site_type is terminal_n because the paper explicitly frames this as the N-terminal extracellular region immediately following the signal peptide \u2014 the canonical position for an N-terminal extracellular tag on a type-I single-pass membrane protein with a long unstructured ectodomain. Full-length TMEM123 construct with ALFA tag inserted after Alanine 34 (8 residues into the mature ectodomain, immediately following the signal peptide cleavage site at residue 26). The construct was expressed and the tag was surface-displayed, confirming extracellular accessibility. [validation: surface_only; position: validated; source: paper; entailment_verified: True]",
      "sources": [
        {
          "citation": "preprint",
          "claim": "The extracellular region of TMEM123 is predicted to be unstructured, so we added the Alfa tag to the N-terminal region following the signal peptide (Alanine 34) (Fig. 4a)."
        }
      ],
